.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt SAFE-FILL-MIGRATE 1
.Os POSIX.1-2017
.Sh NAME
.Nm safe-fill-migrate
.Nd migrate Firefox saved passwords into safe
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl mnf
.Op Fl U Ar user
.Op Fl s Ar safe-dir
.Ar profile-dir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
decrypts the saved logins of the Firefox profile
.Ar profile-dir
using the keys already present in its
.Pa key4.db ,
and stores each password in
.Xr safe 1
under
.Pa passwords/<host>/<user> .
Firefox's store is only read; the passwords stay there until you
remove them in Firefox
.Pq Ql about:logins
once fills from safe work.
.Pp
Two stores are read:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa logins.json
Desktop Firefox.
Each username and password is decrypted with NSS's secret decoder
ring
.Pq Fn PK11SDR_Decrypt ,
using the profile's default key.
.It Pa logins.db
The SQLite store of Mozilla's application-services logins component,
encrypted with the
.Dq as-logins-key
in
.Pa key4.db .
.El
.Pp
.Pa logins.db
is used if it holds any live login; otherwise
.Pa logins.json
is.
A profile can have an empty
.Pa logins.db
next to the
.Pa logins.json
that holds its logins.
If the profile has a Firefox primary password, it is asked for on the
terminal.
.Pp
Expects a running
.Xr safe-agent 1
with the store's key loaded, so that every
.Xr safe 1
invocation goes through the agent and the master password is not
asked for.
.Nm
does not enforce this: if the agent is locked, or serves a different
store,
.Xr safe 1
falls back to prompting for the master password on the terminal, once
per entry.
Unlock the agent first.
The key never appears on the command line or in the process environment.
.Pp
Entries are skipped, and counted as skipped, when they can not be
represented as an entry that
.Xr safe-fill-host 1
can fill:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
plain-http origins (loopback excepted);
.It
logins with an empty username (reported as
.Dq parse failed ) ;
.It
origins or usernames that fail the checks in
.Xr safe-fill-host 1 ,
for example IPv6 literal origins or usernames containing
.Ql / ;
.It
entries that do not decrypt with the profile's key.
.El
.Pp
Origins are lowercased and the default port is removed, so
.Ql https://Example.org:443
becomes
.Pa passwords/example.org/<user> .
.Pp
In the hardened profile,
.Nm
runs as the invoking user and execs
.Xr safe 1
as
.Em _safe-browser
through
.Xr doas 1
via
.Fl U ;
see
.Sx HARDENED PROFILE
below.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl m
Migrate: decrypt every saved login and write it to
.Xr safe 1 .
The only mode; accepted for compatibility.
The former blank mode,
.Fl b ,
was removed and is refused.
.It Fl n
Dry run.
Prints what would be migrated without writing to
.Xr safe 1 .
.It Fl f
Overwrite an existing
.Xr safe 1
entry.
Without
.Fl f ,
an entry that already exists causes that row to be reported as failed.
.It Fl U Ar user
Switch to
.Ar user
for the
.Xr safe 1
invocations: each is exec'd through
.Xr doas 1
with
.Dv SAFE_SOCK
preserved by the doas rule's
.Ql setenv { SAFE_SOCK }
directive
.Po
.Ql keepenv { SAFE_SOCK }
on doas versions that need that spelling
.Pc .
.Nm
runs
.Ql doas
from
.Ev PATH ;
.Pa scripts/safe-fill-migrate-hardened
puts a doas-compatible shim around
.Ql sudo -n
there when doas is not installed, which needs
.Ql Defaults!/usr/local/bin/safe env_keep += \(dqSAFE_SOCK\(dq
in sudoers.
Required in the hardened profile, where the browser store is owned by
.Em _safe-browser
while
.Nm
itself runs as the invoking user and needs that user's access to
.Pa logins.db .
The doas rule
.Ql permit persist setenv { SAFE_SOCK } :wheel as _safe-browser cmd /usr/local/bin/safe
must be present;
without it every
.Xr safe 1
invocation fails and every row is reported as failed.
With
.Fl U ,
.Nm
skips the parent-side ownership and permission checks on
.Dv SAFE_SOCK
and its directory: the socket is owned by the target user, and its
parent directory is not traversable by the invoking user.
The doas child re-validates the socket after the uid switch.
.It Fl s Ar safe-dir
Directory holding the
.Xr safe 1
store.
If omitted, falls back to
.Ev SAFE_DIR ,
then to the compiled-in default.
.El
.Sh HARDENED PROFILE
In the hardened profile the browser agent runs as
.Em _safe-browser ,
its store lives at
.Pa /var/lib/safe/browser ,
and its socket is at
.Pa /run/safe/browser/agent.sock .
.Nm
cannot run as
.Em _safe-browser
directly: it must read the Firefox profile under the invoking user's
home, which
.Em _safe-browser
cannot traverse.
Nor can it run as the invoking user alone: that user cannot write into
.Pa /var/lib/safe/browser
or connect to the agent socket, both of which belong to
.Em _safe-browser .
.Pp
The two-uid split is handled inside
.Nm :
every
.Xr safe 1
subprocess is exec'd through
.Xr doas 1
.Fl u Ar user ,
so the parser, the NSS key extraction, and the
.Pa logins.db
writes all run as the invoking user, while the
.Xr safe 1
read/write of the store and the agent connection all run as
.Em _safe-browser .
The caller provides
.Dv SAFE_SOCK
and the doas rule preserves it across the uid switch.
.Pp
The wrapper
.Pa scripts/safe-fill-migrate-hardened
sets
.Dv SAFE_SOCK No to Pa /run/safe/browser/agent.sock ,
passes
.Fl U Ar _safe-browser
and
.Fl s Ar /var/lib/safe/browser ,
and forwards
.Fl m , Fl n , Fl f
to
.Nm .
It rejects caller-supplied
.Fl U
and
.Fl s
so the hardened invocation cannot be redirected.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "SAFE_SOCK"
.It Ev SAFE_DIR
Location of the
.Xr safe 1
store.
Overridden by
.Fl s .
.It Ev SAFE_SOCK
Path to the UNIX-domain socket bound by
.Xr safe-agent 1 .
Required; the tool refuses to run without it.
In the hardened profile this must be
.Pa /run/safe/browser/agent.sock ;
the doas rule's
.Ql setenv { SAFE_SOCK }
is what preserves it across the uid switch.
.It Ev SAFE_PID
Printed in the header for informational purposes.
Optional.
.It Ev SAFE_BIN
Path to the
.Xr safe 1
binary.
If unset,
.Nm
searches
.Ev PATH
for a
.Pa safe
that is a regular executable owned by root or by the calling user
and not writable by group or others.
.It Ev SAFE_UNSAFE_MLOCK
If set, downgrade
.Xr mlock 2
failures for the key and plaintext buffer from fatal to warning.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa <profile-dir>/logins.json
Desktop Firefox's password store.
Only read.
.It Pa <profile-dir>/logins-backup.json
Firefox's own backup of
.Pa logins.json ;
it holds the passwords too.
Not read.
.It Pa <profile-dir>/logins.db
Application-services password store.
Only read.
.It Pa <profile-dir>/key4.db
NSS key database.
Only read.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Dry-run the migration, leaving Firefox open:

.Dl $ safe-fill-migrate -n ~/.config/mozilla/firefox/*.default/
.Pp
Migrate every entry to
.Xr safe 1
without touching Firefox:

.Dl $ safe-fill-migrate ~/.config/mozilla/firefox/*.default/
.Pp
Same, in the hardened profile, via the wrapper:

.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ safe-unlock browser
$ safe-fill-migrate-hardened ~/.mozilla/firefox/*.default-release
.Ed
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The migration only copies the passwords: they stay in Firefox's
store, in
.Pa logins-backup.json ,
and in any synced copy on the Sync server or other devices until you
remove them in Firefox.
.Pp
Plaintext passwords exist in memory only for the duration of one
.Xr safe 1
invocation each and never touch disk.
The key and plaintext buffer are
.Xr mlock 2 Ns 'd;
core dumps are disabled.
.Pp
.Nm
must never be installed setuid.
A compromised
.Nm
leaks Firefox passwords, which is already the baseline; it has no
privilege the invoking user does not have.
The setuid design is confined to
.Xr safe-fill-host 1 ,
whose attack surface is exactly the native messaging frame.
.Pp
With
.Fl U ,
the
.Xr safe 1
subprocesses get the environment doas or sudo builds for the target
user, plus
.Dv SAFE_SOCK ,
which the rule preserves explicitly.
.Pp
The doas or sudoers rule that
.Fl U
needs lets any process running as the invoking user run
.Xr safe 1
as
.Em _safe-browser
with any arguments, and so decrypt or overwrite browser entries
through the agent without the master password.
Remove the rule after the migration if that matters to you.
.Pp
Granting
.Xr doas 1
the
.Ql env
command instead of
.Ql setenv { SAFE_SOCK } ,
or permitting
.Ql env
in sudoers, would let the invoking user run any command as
.Em _safe-browser ,
which defeats the compartmentalization.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr safe 1 ,
.Xr safe-agent 1 ,
.Xr safe-fill-host 1 ,
.Xr safe-store 5